@@@ problem 48 rotate-image
title: Rotate Image
difficulty: Medium
topics: Array, Math, Matrix
params: matrix
time: O(n²)
space: O(1)
bench: n=64..1048576 time=n space=1 unit=number_of_cells

=== description
Rotate the `n x n` matrix by **90 degrees clockwise**, **in place**. Do not allocate another 2D matrix.

=== constraints
- `n == matrix.length == matrix[i].length`
- `1 <= n <= 20`
- `-1000 <= matrix[i][j] <= 1000`

=== hints
- A clockwise rotation = transpose, then reverse every row.
- Alternatively rotate four cells at a time, layer by layer.

=== template
class Solution {
public:
    void rotate(vector<vector<int>>& matrix) {

    }
};

=== driver
LC_DRIVER {
    h.solve(&Solution::rotate).print_arg(0);
    h.gen([](lc::Gen &g, int n) {
        int side = std::max(1, (int)std::lround(std::sqrt((double)n)));
        std::vector<std::vector<int>> m(side);
        for (auto &row : m) row = g.ints(side, -1000, 1000);
        return lc::lines(m);
    });
}

=== approach
Rotating clockwise by 90° maps cell `(i, j)` to `(j, n-1-i)`. That map is the composition of two simple in-place operations:

1. **transpose**: swap `matrix[i][j]` with `matrix[j][i]` for `j > i`;
2. **reverse each row**.

Both touch every cell a constant number of times and need no extra matrix. (For an n×n matrix this is O(n²) = O(number of cells).)

=== solution
class Solution {
public:
    void rotate(vector<vector<int>>& matrix) {
        int n = matrix.size();
        // 1) transpose across the main diagonal
        for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i)
            for (int j = i + 1; j < n; ++j)
                swap(matrix[i][j], matrix[j][i]);
        // 2) mirror each row horizontally
        for (auto &row : matrix) reverse(row.begin(), row.end());
    }
};

=== examples
[[1,2,3],[4,5,6],[7,8,9]]
> [[7,4,1],[8,5,2],[9,6,3]]

[[5,1,9,11],[2,4,8,10],[13,3,6,7],[15,14,12,16]]
> [[15,13,2,5],[14,3,4,1],[12,6,8,9],[16,7,10,11]]

